WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.450
Now you will write a short transaction using the
earlier example as

00:00:03.475 --> 00:00:07.700
a pattern The task is to temporarily change the age
for MRN

00:00:07.725 --> 00:00:11.525
one zero zero five to fifty-nine inspect the change
with RETURNING roll

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:15.900
it back and then verify that the original age is
restored Start

00:00:15.925 --> 00:00:20.438
with BEGIN followed by a semicolon Then write an
UPDATE statement for

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:26.012
the patients table Use SET age equals fifty-nine Use
a WHERE clause

00:00:26.038 --> 00:00:30.462
that targets MRN one zero zero five Add RETURNING so
you can

00:00:30.488 --> 00:00:34.975
see the row that was changed After the update write
ROLLBACK followed

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.250
by a semicolon The final SELECT is there to verify
the result

00:00:39.275 --> 00:00:43.212
after the rollback Do not rush to type the full
statement from

00:00:43.238 --> 00:00:47.788
memory Look back at the earlier transaction slide
and reuse the same

00:00:47.812 --> 00:00:53.662
structure That is normal programming and database
work Professionals reuse

00:00:53.688 --> 00:00:57.838
known patterns all the time When you run the
completed code you

00:00:57.862 --> 00:01:00.788
should see the temporary age from the UPDATE and
then the original

00:01:00.812 --> 00:01:05.012
age again in the final SELECT The important lesson
is the workflow

00:01:05.275 --> 00:01:09.238
make the change inside a transaction inspect it undo
it and verify

00:01:10.125 --> 00:01:13.388
This pattern gives you a safe way to practice DML
while building

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:14.075
confidence
